Content-Description: CDL: the 2 x 2 of rule types from two flags, the boot kernel's C primitives classified, corrections recorded; proof omi-axioms I-231; CC BY-SA 4.0

"CDL":
  title: "CDL: the Compiler Description Language, and the rule that changes nothing unless it succeeds"
  term: "CDL, Cornelis H. A. Koster, University of Nijmegen, 1971. CDL2, 1976, kept CDL1's principles and added modules, data types, and data change only on success. CDL3 followed (Koster and colleagues, manual 1.2.7, Radboud University)"
  structure: "CDL is built on affix grammars. A rule is a set of alternatives, each a sequence of rule invocations, tried in order; a rule succeeds if one alternative succeeds and fails otherwise. Primitive rules (macros) are written in a host language such as Pascal or C"
  flags: "CDL2 asks the programmer to declare two properties of every rule: can it fail, and can it have a side effect. The four rule types are the cells of that 2 x 2: can fail, no side effect: test; can fail, side effect: predicate; cannot fail, no side effect: function; cannot fail, side effect: action. The flags are the derivation; the four names index the cells"
  directions: "parameters are declared input, output or input-output; three directions by four types is twelve cells. Reading the twelve as the mod-12 ring is the author's reading, not a derivation"
  primitives: "the C primitives of the boot kernel (boxed omi_genesis.c), classified by the two flags, read off the code: the texture and density checks can fail and change nothing: test. atomic_compare_exchange_strong can fail and writes the shared object only on success: predicate. atomic_load cannot fail and changes nothing: function. atomic_store cannot fail and writes: action"
  exchange: "the compare-exchange is CDL2's data change only on success, with one difference between hosts: C11 (7.17.7.4) writes the object's current value into the caller's expected when the compare fails, so the shared object is untouched but the caller's local copy changes; JavaScript's Atomics.compareExchange (boxedPINE space/src/exchange.ts: Atomics.compareExchange(svg, 60 ^ frame, 127, 63)) returns the old value and changes nothing on failure"
  literal: "the ASCII code points the boot kernel uses: 59 is ;, 60 is <, 63 is ?, 127 is DEL. BRIAN XOR CODEX XOR DEEPS = 45 58 48 54 45 = EXHTE, a tag (I-207), so the four tags XOR to zero: a closure of four. The testbed's space.test.ts passes 35 of 35 (2026-10-27)"
  reading: "the labels Day 3 and Day 5; the four code points as the layers CDL, WSN, ALGOL 60 and Plan 9; the closure of four as the tetrahedron's vertices; the compare-exchange as the modal operator the strict logic builds on (the author, 2026-10-27: this is the modal that the strict logic builds on)"
  corrections: "recorded from earlier conversations: CDL is Koster's (1971), not C. H. Lindsey's; the construct ... is ... syntax shown for CDL was invented (CDL uses affix grammar rules); a total order on a fixed set is a line, not a cycle; stars and bars counts distributions of identical items into bins, not ordered sequences of cells; BRIAN XOR CODEX XOR DEEPS = EXHTE is a closure of four, not a Steiner or Fano triple (a Fano line is three points whose XOR is zero); I-229's verdict compares prefixes, not bits, so MIRROR is not literally XNOR; the kernel's stateA XOR stateB == 0 tests equality, which is not I-229's empty-base guard"
  unreachable: "in the omi_genesis.c proposed on 2026-10-26, the Day 5 / Index 63 transition requires texture16 odd; texture counts the bit flips around a closed loop of 16 bits, which is always even, so the condition holds for 0 of 65,536 values and Index 63 can never fire. The Day 3 / Index 59 condition (even texture, density 8) holds for 12,870 values, C(16, 8): every value with 8 bits set. Checked by compiling the two conditions over all 16-bit values"
  proof: "omi-axioms js/catalog/I-231.ts, tag 6F (commit 4febd46, merged 3d7ee34): the four primitives run on a shared buffer, their flags observed, the exchange law checked for all 128 frames, and the Day 5 condition counted; seven predictions filed before the run, all hold"
  state: "checked"
  sources: "Wikipedia, Compiler Description Language and Cornelis H. A. Koster (CC BY-SA 4.0, so this article is CC BY-SA 4.0); Koster et al., CDL3 manual 1.2.7, ftp.cs.ru.nl/cdl3 (cited, not quoted); ISO C11 7.17.7.4; boxedPINE space/src/exchange.ts and src/testbed/space.test.ts; omi-axioms js/catalog/I-207.ts"
